{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.LiftingDescent.FrobeniusNormRoute","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.FrobeniusNormRoute","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Norm.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.ZMod.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Prime.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.Zsqrtd.GaussianInt"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.one_add_i_not_isUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.one_add_i_not_isUnit","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L170-L181","doc":"**Half two: the norm route goes through anyway.**  `1 + i` is a non-unit of\n`ℤ[i]`, established through `Zsqrtd.normMonoidHom : ℤ√d →* ℤ` and the\ncriterion above — in a ring that half one has just shown admits *no* ring\nhom to `ℤ`.\n\nWorth noting what carries it: `IsUnit` is not decidable here, so `decide`\ncannot prove this directly (it fails to synthesise `Decidable ¬IsUnit _`).\nThe `decide` discharges only `1 < |N(1+i)| = 2`; the criterion does the\nrest.  So the norm is not decoration — it is what makes the statement\nreachable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.no_ringHom_gaussianInt_to_int","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.no_ringHom_gaussianInt_to_int","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L154-L168","doc":"**Half one: the ring-hom route is blocked, and this is a proof, not a\ncitation.**  There is no ring homomorphism `ℤ[i] →+* ℤ` at all — `i` would\nhave to go to an integer square root of `-1`, and a square in `ℤ` is\nnon-negative.\n\nThis is the computable analogue of `qou-lrnd`'s finding that no\n`ℤ[ζ_2p] →+* ℤ` exists (there, because `Φ_2p` has no rational root).  Same\nobstruction, same reason: a ring hom must send a generator to a root of its\nminimal polynomial, and there is none in `ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.gaussianInt_i_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.gaussianInt_i_sq","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L151-L152","doc":"`i² = -1`, computed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.proton_residue_isUnit_at_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.proton_residue_isUnit_at_three","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L134-L139","doc":"**The gap is concrete, not hypothetical.**  At the proton collapse constant\n`χ(A) = 8` (as measured, per `qou-lrnd`'s 2026-08-23 correction) and `p = 3`,\nthe residue is a unit — so by `not_isUnit_iff_eq_zero_groupWithZero` the residue\nroute provably concludes nothing there, while a norm bound would. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.not_isUnit_iff_eq_zero_groupWithZero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.not_isUnit_iff_eq_zero_groupWithZero","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L117-L132","doc":"**Why the residue route was capped, as a theorem.**\n\nIn a group with zero — a field, and in particular `𝔽_p` — a non-unit is\nexactly zero.  So a residue map into such a target can witness non-unitness\n*only* by vanishing; there is no third option, and a congruence to a\nnonzero constant therefore cannot witness it at any prime where the\nconstant survives.\n\nStated at `GroupWithZero` rather than at `ZMod p` on purpose: the cap comes\nfrom *every nonzero element being invertible*, not from anything about\nresidues or primes.  `qou-lrnd` records the `ZMod` instance of this as\n`isUnit_four_zmod` and the reasoning as prose; this is the general fact\nboth are shadows of, and it is what `ℤ` — not a group with zero — escapes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.frobenius_not_unit_of_norm_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.frobenius_not_unit_of_norm_bound","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L97-L115","doc":"**The perpetual shape.**  Note there is no exception set: the conclusion is\nat *every* prime, not at all but finitely many.\n\nThis is the structural payoff over the collapse route.  A congruence to a\nfixed constant `C` could only ever give non-unitness at the primes dividing\n`C` — finitely many, \"the complement of what perpetual means\" (`qou-lrnd`).\nA norm bound holding at every prime gives the obstruction at every prime.\n\n🛑 **The hypothesis has NO valid instantiation at the QOU Frobenius**\n(measured 2026-08-25; see the file header).  `N(Frob_3) = 1` for both\nnucleons, so `∀ p, 1 < |N (frob p)|` is false and this theorem, while true,\ncannot be applied to that family as stated.  It is kept because the\nimplication is correct and is the form a caller with a genuine norm bound\nwants — but do not read it as a route the QOU Frobenius takes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.not_isUnit_of_algebraNorm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.not_isUnit_of_algebraNorm","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L88-L95","doc":"**The criterion at the actual algebraic norm**, so it is not merely an\nabstract shape.  `Algebra.norm ℤ` is a `MonoidHom S →* ℤ` by definition.\nThe `Module.Free` / `Module.Finite` hypotheses are what make it the genuine\nfield norm rather than the junk value the definition falls back to. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.LiftingDescent.not_isUnit_of_natAbs_norm_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.LiftingDescent.not_isUnit_of_natAbs_norm_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/LiftingDescent/FrobeniusNormRoute.lean#L74-L86","doc":"**Non-unitness transfers backwards along any multiplicative map to `ℤ`.**\n\nNothing here is about number fields: a unit maps to a unit, the units of\n`ℤ` are `±1`, so a norm of absolute value `> 1` forces a non-unit.  Stated\nfor a bare `Monoid` and a `MonoidHom` precisely because that is the\ngenerality the norm lives in — see the file header for why the ring-hom\nversion is unavailable. ","signatureChunk":0}]}